# EA "real" kernel config delta — the FAT kernel that runs the system.
#
# openHC EA boot is two kernels (see board/ea/common/rootfs-overlay/etc/init.d/
# S00kexec and the KERNEL SIZE BUDGET in linux/common.fragment):
#
#   * BOOTSTRAP — the bzImage in the CEFDK slot. Must stay under CEFDK
#     bootlinux's 7,417,856-byte copy window, so it is kept lean (DRM/SGX/etc.
#     as modules). Its only job is to reach p1 (or the network) and kexec the
#     real kernel. That is the kernel build.sh produces normally.
#
#   * REAL — kexec'd from /boot/bzImage on p1. kexec has NO size limit, so this
#     kernel builds the large subsystems straight IN and never fights the 7 MB
#     wall again. This fragment is what makes it fat; it is merged LAST (after
#     common + the board file + every feature fragment), so each line here wins.
#
# This retires the pvrsrvkm rmmod/insmod dance: with the GPU built in, it is
# simply present when the real kernel boots. New EA functionality can now go in
# as =y here without the size check in post-image.sh ever being in the way.
#
# NOTE: merge_config scrapes "SYMBOL=" even inside comments, so refer to symbols
# without an assignment in prose.

# --- GPU: PowerVR SGX545, built IN (was =m in features/sgx to save bzImage) ---
# CONFIG_DRM built in pulls the DRM core + KMS/display helpers (~1.25 MiB) into
# the kernel; that weight is the whole reason it was a module, and the whole
# reason no longer applies here. SGX545_CE depends on DRM and follows it to =y;
# its objs.mk line is obj-$(CONFIG_SGX545_CE), so =y builds the driver in.
CONFIG_DRM=y
CONFIG_SGX545_CE=y

# --- framebuffer display built IN (ce5300-fb via FB_SIMPLE) ---
# ce5300-fb.o is obj-$(CONFIG_FB_SIMPLE); keep the panel/console path built in on
# the real kernel exactly as on the bootstrap. (Harmless if already set.)
CONFIG_FB=y
CONFIG_FB_SIMPLE=y

# Modules stay ON: this kernel can still carry loadable modules (the ASoC audio
# stack is still obj-m in objs.mk and loads from p1). Nothing here forces them
# off — the point is only that the LARGE, always-present subsystems are built in
# so the size ceiling stops dictating the design.
CONFIG_MODULES=y

# --- KEEP THE IOMMU OFF (or the eMMC dies) -------------------------------------
# Building DRM in pulls CONFIG_IOMMU_SUPPORT, and on x86 INTEL_IOMMU then defaults
# =y — so the fat kernel, unlike the lean slot kernel, came up with Intel VT-d DMA
# remapping active. This SoC's VT-d (Cedarview / CE5300, 2011-era Atom) is buggy:
# with it on, the SDHCI controller's ADMA transfers hit an empty translation and
# the eMMC never initialises (on a live EA1: "mmc0: sdhci: ADMA Err: 0x00000001",
# "mmc0: Timeout waiting for hardware interrupt", "error -110 whilst initialising
# MMC card"). With rootwait the box then hangs forever before mounting p1 — i.e.
# the fat kernel bricks the boot for a reason that has nothing to do with the GPU.
# The slot kernel never had VT-d, so match it: no IOMMU driver at all. (S00kexec
# also appends intel_iommu=off on the kexec cmdline as a belt-and-suspenders.)
# CONFIG_INTEL_IOMMU is not set
# CONFIG_INTEL_IOMMU_DEFAULT_ON is not set

# No module signing. Secure boot is OFF on this hardware (the bootstrap kernel
# signs nothing either), so there is no signing key and nothing to enforce.
# CONFIG_MODULE_SIG is not set
# CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_ALL is not set
#
# NOTE: the trusted keyring / SYSTEM_DATA_VERIFICATION are NOT disabled here. They
# are SELECTed by other enabled options, not user-settable, so a "# ... is not
# set" line for them only produces a merge_config "redefined" warning and is
# ignored. They pull in certs/extract-cert, a HOST tool — mk-real-kernel.sh builds
# it against the runner's own openssl (HOSTPKG_CONFIG) so it links cleanly.
